#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f" :knowledge-point/title "Projecting a Vector Onto a Perpendicular Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bcf84f5-af7e-5e1c-b912-1c3500934064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bcf84f5-af7e-5e1c-b912-1c3500934064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275935} :answer-field/id #uuid "37da1b28-1191-5323-840a-68f8d18e3a4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "693153e1-7945-56bf-830b-01e6c2d76a16" :question/math-academy-id "q-319171" :question/problem "Let $p=⟨4,1,-2⟩$ and $q=⟨1,x,3⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+1\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
